1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,630
On the Monday of his second week, Albert Wesker
called a meeting, and told nine people

2
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:17,099
what the company they worked for
was going to sell.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,421
They had been expecting a strategy session. What they
got was closer to a weather report:

4
00:00:23,441 --> 00:00:29,030
brief, factual, and about a storm that had already
been decided upon somewhere else.

5
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:36,030
We make diagnostics. Specifically, we make one: a
pathogen panel. Blood or swab in, forty-one

6
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:42,321
organisms out, under twenty minutes, on a benchtop box
a clinic in Ohio can afford without

7
00:00:42,341 --> 00:00:42,880
a grant.

8
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:49,600
A hand went up. One of Voss's juniors, twenty-six
years old, brave for four more seconds.

9
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:53,310
Director — that's a crowded market.
Everyone has a panel.

10
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:58,317
Everyone has a panel that takes ninety minutes,
costs four hundred dollars a run, and

11
00:00:58,337 --> 00:01:02,944
is wrong about coinfections. Ours will take
nineteen minutes, cost thirty-one dollars

12
00:01:02,964 --> 00:01:04,522
a run, and will not be wrong.

13
00:01:04,924 --> 00:01:06,054
How do you know?

14
00:01:06,453 --> 00:01:12,609
And Wesker looked at him, for rather longer than was
comfortable, with the flat patience

15
00:01:12,629 --> 00:01:15,908
of a man watching a dog work out
a door handle.

16
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:21,215
Because I have read the literature, Mr. Aldridge, and
because I have read what the literature

17
00:01:21,235 --> 00:01:22,920
was careful not to say. Sit down.

18
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:31,938
Blueprints. Rapid Pathogen Assay, a forty-one target
panel: two thousand five hundred.

19
00:01:31,958 --> 00:01:40,132
Stable Protein Scaffold Library: two thousand two
hundred. Veterinary Antiviral: fifteen

20
00:01:40,152 --> 00:01:40,876
hundred.

21
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:42,028
Purchase.

22
00:01:43,058 --> 00:01:50,991
Purchase. Rapid Pathogen Assay. Two thousand five
hundred. Balance, two thousand four

23
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:55,202
hundred. Delivered to Sublevel
B4, bench two.

24
00:01:55,792 --> 00:02:01,157
It did not appear in a flash of light. It appeared
the way everything appeared: as though

25
00:02:01,177 --> 00:02:02,912
it had been there for a year.

26
00:02:03,311 --> 00:02:09,837
Six binders of process documentation. Validation data
from two hundred and eleven clinical

27
00:02:09,857 --> 00:02:16,527
samples. A manufacturing specification down to the
grade of the plastic. A cartridge design.

28
00:02:16,547 --> 00:02:23,000
A reagent formulation. Three years of somebody's
meticulous work, in a lab that had never

29
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:26,781
done it, signed off by scientists
who did not exist.

30
00:02:27,181 --> 00:02:31,974
Wesker read all of it in a night, which is
not a figure of speech.

31
00:02:32,375 --> 00:02:35,594
And then, because he was who he
was, he improved it.

32
00:02:35,995 --> 00:02:42,375
The System's design ran its reaction in twenty-two
minutes and called it twenty. The limiting

33
00:02:42,395 --> 00:02:48,639
step was the lysis: getting a virus or a bacterium
open fast enough to read what was inside

34
00:02:48,659 --> 00:02:51,805
it without destroying what you
wanted to read.

35
00:02:52,205 --> 00:02:58,534
The blueprint used heat and a chaotropic salt.
Everybody used heat and a chaotropic salt.

36
00:02:59,055 --> 00:03:04,596
And everybody has the same three failures. Enveloped
virus at low titre. Mycobacteria.

37
00:03:04,616 --> 00:03:05,048
Spores.

38
00:03:05,449 --> 00:03:11,299
He spent four nights on it, alone, with the vault open
and a rack of the archive's stranger

39
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:16,846
cell lines thawing beside him, and on the fourth
night he cut the reaction to nineteen

40
00:03:16,866 --> 00:03:22,781
minutes by doing something that no competitor's chemist
would have dared put in a regulatory

41
00:03:22,801 --> 00:03:26,199
filing: he stopped trying to open
everything at once.

42
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:32,486
Two chambers instead of one. A gentle enzymatic step
for the fragile organisms, running

43
00:03:32,506 --> 00:03:38,663
in parallel with a brutal mechanical one for the tough
ones, and the two streams recombined

44
00:03:38,683 --> 00:03:44,501
at the amplification stage with an internal control
that corrected for the difference.

45
00:03:44,521 --> 00:03:50,679
It was inelegant. It was, in the phrase Voss used when
she saw it, *offensively practical.*

46
00:03:50,699 --> 00:03:56,653
And it made the panel correct about the exact cases
that every other panel on the market

47
00:03:56,673 --> 00:04:00,929
got wrong: the patient with two
infections, one of them hiding.

48
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:03,099
Who thinks like this?

49
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:06,707
Someone who has been on the other end
of a false negative.

50
00:04:07,106 --> 00:04:12,695
He said it without any particular weight, and Dr.
Lena Voss did not ask what he meant,

51
00:04:12,715 --> 00:04:18,433
and the narrator will simply note that Albert Wesker
had once, in another world, watched

52
00:04:18,453 --> 00:04:24,172
a containment protocol fail because a test came back
clean on a Tuesday, and that he had

53
00:04:24,192 --> 00:04:28,736
learned from it, and that the thing he had
learned was not compassion.

54
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:38,464
Patent filed. Two-stream lysis with cross-normalised
internal control. Inventor: A. Wesker.

55
00:04:38,484 --> 00:04:42,999
Acting with intelligence: twelve
hundred Points.

56
00:04:43,588 --> 00:04:47,999
Mr. Pell did the rest, and Mr. Pell, it
turned out, was a kind of weapon.

57
00:04:48,399 --> 00:04:54,129
He never raised his voice. He never appeared to
hurry. He arrived each morning at seven

58
00:04:54,149 --> 00:05:00,011
twelve with a folder, and over six weeks that folder
quietly turned a certified blueprint

59
00:05:00,031 --> 00:05:01,069
into a business.

60
00:05:01,588 --> 00:05:06,323
The device qualifies as a moderate-complexity panel.
There is a predicate on the market,

61
00:05:06,343 --> 00:05:10,970
which means clearance rather than approval: four
months, not four years. I have filed.

62
00:05:10,990 --> 00:05:15,779
The submission is complete and correct and there is
nothing in it for a reviewer to want.

63
00:05:16,178 --> 00:05:17,204
Manufacturing.

64
00:05:17,603 --> 00:05:22,534
Contracted in Puerto Rico for the first eighteen
months, at a price that is high because

65
00:05:22,554 --> 00:05:27,317
we are unknown. I have taken the high price and a
clause that lets us leave on thirty

66
00:05:27,337 --> 00:05:30,973
days' notice, because we will not be
unknown for eighteen months.

67
00:05:31,374 --> 00:05:32,411
Price to the customer.

68
00:05:32,810 --> 00:05:38,079
The market is three hundred and eighty dollars a run.
Our cost at volume is nine. I would

69
00:05:38,099 --> 00:05:39,920
suggest one hundred and ninety.

70
00:05:40,321 --> 00:05:41,032
Thirty-one.

71
00:05:41,432 --> 00:05:42,642
Pell's pen stopped.

72
00:05:43,161 --> 00:05:48,250
Director. At thirty-one dollars we make twenty-two
dollars a cartridge instead of a hundred

73
00:05:48,270 --> 00:05:49,092
and eighty-one.

74
00:05:49,492 --> 00:05:53,937
At thirty-one dollars every hospital network in the
mid-Atlantic replaces its instruments

75
00:05:53,957 --> 00:05:58,402
inside a year, and the instrument they replace them
with is ours, and the cartridges only

76
00:05:58,422 --> 00:06:02,767
fit ours, and in four years there is no such thing
as a three-hundred-and-eighty-dollar

77
00:06:02,787 --> 00:06:07,081
panel because I will have made the price of being
wrong about a patient too obvious to

78
00:06:07,101 --> 00:06:07,432
defend.

79
00:06:08,052 --> 00:06:10,023
(flat) Thirty-one dollars.

80
00:06:10,423 --> 00:06:16,077
And, Mr. Pell — at cost to any public health
laboratory that asks. Every one of them.

81
00:06:16,097 --> 00:06:19,215
No paperwork, no negotiation, no
press release.

82
00:06:19,615 --> 00:06:22,925
That is a considerable amount of
money to give away.

83
00:06:23,324 --> 00:06:28,037
It is a considerable amount of money to *lend*. They
will have our boxes on their benches

84
00:06:28,057 --> 00:06:32,770
when something frightening happens, and when
something frightening happens they will call

85
00:06:32,790 --> 00:06:33,939
the number on the box.

86
00:06:34,338 --> 00:06:40,288
The narrator notes, for the record, that the first
genuinely evil thing Albert Wesker

87
00:06:40,308 --> 00:06:46,539
did in this world was to give away a very good
medical test to the people who most needed

88
00:06:46,559 --> 00:06:49,489
it, for free, on purpose, ten years early.

89
00:06:49,889 --> 00:06:55,609
The clearance came through on the fourth of
November. The first orders came in on the

90
00:06:55,629 --> 00:07:01,620
ninth. On the twelfth, a man named Graham Tolliver
drove up from Trenton with his general

91
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:07,563
counsel and asked for a meeting, and was given one at
four o'clock, and was kept waiting

92
00:07:07,583 --> 00:07:08,779
until four twenty.

93
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:15,158
Tolliver was the chief executive of Verdant Axis
Therapeutics: thirteen hundred employees,

94
00:07:15,178 --> 00:07:21,223
a listed stock, and a syndromic panel that had taken
his company nine years and two hundred

95
00:07:21,243 --> 00:07:27,422
million dollars to build. It ran in ninety minutes and
cost three hundred and eighty dollars,

96
00:07:27,442 --> 00:07:31,888
and until the fourth of November it had
been the best in its class.

97
00:07:32,288 --> 00:07:37,614
He had come to buy the patent. He had a number in his
pocket and a threat behind it, and

98
00:07:37,634 --> 00:07:39,619
he had rehearsed both in the car.

99
00:07:40,019 --> 00:07:44,409
Wesker came in at four twenty,
alone, with no folder.

100
00:07:44,928 --> 00:07:46,266
Mr. Tolliver.

101
00:07:46,668 --> 00:07:48,578
You've kept me waiting twenty minutes.

102
00:07:48,978 --> 00:07:49,353
Yes.

103
00:07:49,752 --> 00:07:55,822
He sat down. He did not apologise and he did not
explain, and the silence that followed

104
00:07:55,842 --> 00:08:02,262
lasted long enough that Tolliver's counsel began the
pitch himself, out of sheer discomfort,

105
00:08:02,282 --> 00:08:04,923
four minutes before he had planned to.

106
00:08:05,442 --> 00:08:10,045
My client is prepared to acquire the two-stream lysis
patent and the associated instrument

107
00:08:10,065 --> 00:08:14,463
line for forty million dollars. To be frank, Dr.
Wesker, that is a generous number for

108
00:08:14,483 --> 00:08:19,137
a company that shipped its first unit nine days ago.
And I should say that we have reviewed

109
00:08:19,157 --> 00:08:23,555
your filings and we believe there are — overlaps —
with our own portfolio, which would

110
00:08:23,575 --> 00:08:26,071
be expensive for a firm of your
size to litigate.

111
00:08:26,471 --> 00:08:30,710
Wesker looked at him with what appeared
to be polite interest.

112
00:08:31,230 --> 00:08:31,883
Which claim?

113
00:08:32,283 --> 00:08:33,193
I'm sorry?

114
00:08:33,594 --> 00:08:38,447
You have asserted an overlap. Tell me which claim of
which patent. You have eight in this

115
00:08:38,467 --> 00:08:43,211
space. Nine of them are amplification chemistry,
which I do not use. One is a cartridge

116
00:08:43,231 --> 00:08:47,920
geometry that expired in March, and one is the
ninety-minute thermal protocol, which I

117
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:50,165
would not want if you paid me to take it.

118
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:56,543
The counsel opened his folder. Wesker had not
brought a folder, and the counsel's was

119
00:08:56,563 --> 00:08:59,170
suddenly the loudest thing in the room.

120
00:08:59,690 --> 00:09:04,205
You came here with a number and a bluff, and the
number is an insult and the bluff is

121
00:09:04,225 --> 00:09:08,847
a question you cannot answer in front of your own
chief executive. Let us not spend the

122
00:09:08,867 --> 00:09:09,701
afternoon on it.

123
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:12,271
Then let's talk seriously. Eighty million.

124
00:09:12,671 --> 00:09:12,814
No.

125
00:09:13,212 --> 00:09:15,702
A hundred and twenty. That's more
than your entire —

126
00:09:16,102 --> 00:09:20,966
Mr. Tolliver. Your Q3 statement shows a hundred and
ninety million in panel revenue, which

127
00:09:20,986 --> 00:09:25,688
is sixty-one per cent of your business, and a debt
covenant that tests against trailing

128
00:09:25,708 --> 00:09:30,354
revenue every quarter. You have already lost the
Christiana network, and you will lose

129
00:09:30,374 --> 00:09:35,238
Jefferson within the month, because I have priced a
better test at eight per cent of yours

130
00:09:35,258 --> 00:09:39,960
and I have no shareholders to explain it to. By
March your trailing revenue will breach

131
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:44,789
that covenant. Your lender will not renegotiate,
because your lender has spent four years

132
00:09:44,809 --> 00:09:49,619
looking for a reason not to. You did not come here to
buy a patent. You came here because

133
00:09:49,639 --> 00:09:52,386
you have a fuse and you have read
the length of it.

134
00:09:52,788 --> 00:09:53,978
Nobody spoke.

135
00:09:54,378 --> 00:09:59,721
The narrator would like to record what was actually
happening in that room, because it

136
00:09:59,741 --> 00:10:05,085
happened at every scale for the next sixty-five
years and the mechanism never changed.

137
00:10:05,105 --> 00:10:10,449
Wesker had not threatened anyone. He had not lied.
He had simply arrived in possession

138
00:10:10,469 --> 00:10:15,813
of every fact about the other man's life, arranged
them on the table in the order that

139
00:10:15,833 --> 00:10:20,928
hurt most, and then waited, without expression,
for the other man to look at them.

140
00:10:21,447 --> 00:10:24,052
I will not sell you the patent. I
will license it to you.

141
00:10:24,451 --> 00:10:25,400
License it.

142
00:10:25,799 --> 00:10:30,696
Non-exclusive, five years, for your instrument line.
It will let you cut your run time

143
00:10:30,716 --> 00:10:35,726
to thirty-one minutes and your price to sixty
dollars, which will save your covenant and

144
00:10:35,746 --> 00:10:40,586
your job. The fee is nine million a year, and four
per cent of the panel revenue, and

145
00:10:40,606 --> 00:10:41,557
one further term.

146
00:10:41,957 --> 00:10:42,788
Which is?

147
00:10:43,187 --> 00:10:48,838
Verdant Axis withdraws from the public-health tender in
Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Delaware.

148
00:10:48,858 --> 00:10:52,096
You will not bid against me for
government work again.

149
00:10:52,495 --> 00:10:58,046
The general counsel started to say something about
restraint of trade. Tolliver put a

150
00:10:58,066 --> 00:11:03,748
hand on his arm without looking at him — the gesture
of a man who has stopped listening

151
00:11:03,768 --> 00:11:09,581
to advice — and stared across the table at a face he
could not see behind a pair of black

152
00:11:09,601 --> 00:11:10,105
glasses.

153
00:11:10,625 --> 00:11:11,915
You're letting me live.

154
00:11:12,316 --> 00:11:16,205
I am letting you *work*. There is a difference, and
you will feel it every quarter when

155
00:11:16,225 --> 00:11:17,149
the payment goes out.

156
00:11:17,548 --> 00:11:23,809
He signed on the twenty-second of November. Verdant
Axis's stock went up eleven per cent

157
00:11:23,829 --> 00:11:30,161
on the announcement of the licence, and Graham
Tolliver was praised in two trade journals

158
00:11:30,181 --> 00:11:31,660
for his decisiveness.

159
00:11:32,809 --> 00:11:41,697
Subjugation of a rival. Verdant Axis Therapeutics:
licensed, constrained, retained. Fourteen

160
00:11:41,717 --> 00:11:48,379
thousand Points. Balance, seventeen
thousand six hundred. Level four.

161
00:11:48,969 --> 00:11:51,829
And the other one, quietly, from the
back of the skull.

162
00:11:52,349 --> 00:11:54,407
You should have taken the company.

163
00:11:54,807 --> 00:11:59,828
I have taken the company. I have merely left him in it
to run it for me, at his own expense,

164
00:11:59,848 --> 00:12:02,020
while he tells everyone it was his idea.

165
00:12:02,419 --> 00:12:05,661
That is not a victory anyone can see.

166
00:12:06,062 --> 00:12:06,542
Correct.

167
00:12:06,942 --> 00:12:11,272
By the middle of December, the boxes were
in ninety-one hospitals.

168
00:12:11,672 --> 00:12:17,064
A charge nurse in Chester told a reporter she had
found two infections in one child on

169
00:12:17,084 --> 00:12:22,665
a Sunday night with nobody senior in the building,
and that the old test would have found

170
00:12:22,685 --> 00:12:28,329
one. A public health laboratory in Dover ran four
hundred samples in a week on instruments

171
00:12:28,349 --> 00:12:34,056
it had not paid for. An infectious disease physician
in Baltimore wrote an editorial titled

172
00:12:34,076 --> 00:12:39,719
*The Thirty-One Dollar Question*, which was not about
Umbrella at all, and which mentioned

173
00:12:39,739 --> 00:12:40,601
Umbrella once.

174
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,971
Nobody, anywhere, was frightened.

175
00:12:43,370 --> 00:12:48,831
On the last night of the year Pell came up with the
folder, as he did every night, and

176
00:12:48,851 --> 00:12:53,610
read the numbers aloud in the flat voice of a
man reciting a shopping list.

177
00:12:54,130 --> 00:12:58,957
Units placed: four hundred and six. Cartridges
shipped in December: two hundred and nine

178
00:12:58,977 --> 00:13:03,914
thousand. Licence income from Verdant Axis: nine
million, first instalment received. Cash:

179
00:13:03,934 --> 00:13:08,761
thirty-one million, four hundred thousand. Headcount:
thirty-eight. Total assets, on the

180
00:13:08,781 --> 00:13:11,681
balance sheet as it will be filed:
fifty-two million.

181
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:17,531
Wesker had been standing at the window with his
back to the room. He turned around.

182
00:13:18,051 --> 00:13:20,957
Read that last figure again, Mr.
Pell. Slowly.

183
00:13:21,355 --> 00:13:23,586
(flat) Fifty-two million dollars.

184
00:13:24,615 --> 00:13:32,906
Main mission, tier two. A product worth selling:
complete. Fifty million dollars in assets:

185
00:13:32,926 --> 00:13:37,015
complete. Fifty staff:
thirty-eight of fifty.

186
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:43,151
Twelve people short. Fifteen weeks from a chair in an
empty office to twelve people short

187
00:13:43,171 --> 00:13:43,777
of a tier.

188
00:13:44,177 --> 00:13:49,827
He looked at the panel for a moment with his hands
behind his back, and then he said the

189
00:13:49,847 --> 00:13:55,561
thing that the System, in all its billions of
candidates and all its criteria, had chosen

190
00:13:55,581 --> 00:13:56,076
him for.

191
00:13:56,596 --> 00:14:01,278
Then we will hire twelve people tomorrow, and the day
after that I would like to discuss

192
00:14:01,298 --> 00:14:01,812
the Gacha.
